# Line endings, and why this file leads with them.
#
# The build is byte-reproducible and its output is committed: CI rebuilds the
# site and fails if anything under docs/ differs from what is in the tree. That
# guarantee is only as good as the bytes git hands the build. On a checkout
# configured with core.autocrlf=true, git would translate every text file here
# on the way out, and the build would then be reading and writing different
# bytes from the ones committed -- while every local build on an LF machine
# stayed clean, which is exactly the kind of failure nobody finds.
#
# One concrete consequence, found in review: ASSET_VERSION in build/render.mjs
# hashes four CSS and JS files to derive the ?v= query on every page. A CRLF
# checkout of the same commit produced a different digest, and 72 generated
# HTML files differed on nothing but that query string.
#
# So every text file the build reads or writes is pinned. This includes the
# build sources themselves: guides.mjs and glossary.mjs hold HTML inside
# template literals, so a CR in one of those files is a CR in the published
# page.
#
# ORDER MATTERS BELOW. The last matching pattern wins, so these broad rules
# come first and the deliberate exceptions after them. Moving the entropy
# artifact's -text lines above these would silently re-enable translation on
# the one file whose exact bytes are published as a checksum.
*.css    text eol=lf
*.js     text eol=lf
*.mjs    text eol=lf
*.html   text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf
*.svg    text eol=lf
*.xml    text eol=lf
*.txt    text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.mts    text eol=lf

# The Entropy Workshop's downloadable artifact and its checksum.
#
# -text means git stores and checks out these bytes exactly, with no
# end-of-line translation in either direction. That is not a preference here,
# it is a correctness requirement: the SHA-256 published beside the artifact
# describes specific bytes, and letting git rewrite them on commit or checkout
# made the published checksum describe a file nobody could obtain. Someone
# following the verify instructions got a mismatch and was right to stop.
#
# !eol unspecifies the eol attribute the *.html rule above would otherwise
# leave set. -text alone is already authoritative -- git performs no
# conversion when text is unset, whatever eol says -- but leaving a stale
# "eol: lf" showing in `git check-attr` output invites someone to remove the
# -text later and assume the file is still protected.
docs/entropy-offline.html        -text !eol
docs/entropy-offline.html.sha256 -text !eol
*.sh text eol=lf

# Markdown is written on Windows and Linux both, and nothing in the build
# reads it, so its line endings are invisible until a diff shows a whole file
# rewritten. That happened once: an editing script wrote LICENSING-AUDIT.md
# back as CRLF with one stray CR, and with core.autocrlf left to decide, those
# bytes went into the blob as written -- 267 insertions and 266 deletions for
# a two-line change.
#
# Every tracked .md blob is LF today. This pins that, so the next edit made on
# a Windows checkout cannot quietly commit a different file.
*.md text eol=lf

# Licence and notice texts, which have no .md extension and so were not covered
# by the rule above. These are reproduced upstream documents: their bytes are
# the point, several are compared against served files by the build's notice
# check, and one is a licence this project grants under. Every one of these
# blobs is LF today, so this pins the current state rather than changing it --
# what it prevents is the same accident the Markdown rule prevents, an edit made
# on a Windows checkout quietly committing a different file.
LICENSE         text eol=lf
COPYING         text eol=lf
LICENSE-*.txt   text eol=lf
OFL-*.txt       text eol=lf
